# What the front-end lint does not read (#14).
#
# Everything tracked in this repository that Prettier understands is checked,
# except one directory of tracked files that exist to be malformed. A tracked
# path that cannot be formatted belongs in this list with the reason on the line
# above it.
#
# THIS FILE ONCE SAID IT EXCLUDED NO TRACKED FILE AND CHANGED NO VERDICT. Both
# sentences stopped being true when the fuzzing seed corpus landed (#18), and
# they are replaced rather than softened: the corpus entry below is a tracked
# path, and removing this file now reds the check.
#
# The other entries are build and measurement output, which git does not track
# either. They are named so that a generated file cannot start deciding this
# check's verdict after somebody builds or runs the suite in their clone.

bin/
obj/
artifacts/

# Where `dotnet test --results-directory` puts the coverage report the floor
# check reads.
TestResults/

# The fuzzing seed corpus (#18). These are inputs rather than documents: the
# whole point of several of them is to be a shape the parser has to refuse, and
# one of them is not valid JSON at all. Formatting them would repair the very
# thing they are there to carry, and the formatter cannot parse the invalid one
# in the first place. A test in the suite reads every file in this directory and
# asserts the parser answers rather than throws, which is the check these files
# owe and it is not a formatting one.
tools/fuzz/corpus/
